    "lawCode": "Amd §202-e, St Ad Proc Act; add §138-a, Ec Dev L",
    "lawSection": "State Administrative Procedure Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6800A\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state administrative procedure act and the economic\ndevelopment law, in relation to requiring the division for small-busi-\nness to publish a small business compliance guide\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is for the Secretary of State to compile\nand publish a small business guide which catalogues the statutory and\nregulatory changes of the previous year in plain and commonly understood\nterms.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n  Section  1-  Amends subdivisions 1 and 2 of section 202-e of the state\nadministrative procedure act to require that annually a  small  business\ncompliance guide be published in the state register.  Additionally, this\nsection  requires  that the small business compliance guide be published\nin the state register regardless of  the  guide's  availability  on  the\nDepartment of State's website.\n\n  Section  2  - The economic development law is amended to include a new\nsection 138-a. Under this section the division  for  Small  Business  is\nrequired to publish a small business compliance guide by January 31st of\neach  year  in  consultation  with any other relevant state agency.  The\nguide will inform small business  owners  of  statutory  and  regulatory\nchanges  that  have  taken  effect  in  the previous year. The guide may\ninclude but is not  limited  to  changes  regarding  permits,  licenses,\ntaxes,  insurance,  workplace  safety,  compensation,  wages, hours, and\nbenefits. The guide will also include the appropriate  contact  informa-\ntion  for  each  state  agency. The guide must describe any statutory or\nregulatory changes in plain and  commonly  understood  terms  and  limit\ntechnical terms whenever possible. The Secretary shall publish the guide\non the department's website. The secretary shall also engage in a public\nawareness  campaign  coordinating  with  relevant statewide and regional\nbusiness advocacy organizations. The public  awareness  campaign  should\neducate  small  business  owners about the availability of the guide and\nother resources available to assist small businesses with compliance.\n\n  Section 3 - Effective Date - One year after this bill shall  become  a\nlaw.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nUnder  the  current  law,  New York State is not statutorily required to\ncreate a small business compliance guide informing  business  owners  of\nstatutory  and  regulatory  changes  in  one easy-to-understand guidance\ndocument.\n\nThe current law requires every agency to  submit  to  the  Secretary  of\nState  a  list  of all guidance documents on which the agency relies and\nhow the public may obtain such documents.  The  law  also  exempts  such\nguidance  documents  from publication in the state register if the guid-\nance documents are available on the agency's website.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSmall  businesses  must  comply with many local, state, and federal laws\nand regulations. Each year the legislature and relevant  state  agencies\nchange  both  the law and corresponding regulations. Changes to the laws\nand regulations are often conveyed in  formal,  technical,  and  complex\njargon.  Keeping  up with these changes can be a burdensome task for any\nbusiness owner. This legislation seeks to ease that burden by collecting\nmany of the statutory and regulatory changes of the previous year  in  a\n\"small  business  compliance  guide\".  This  guide will take complex and\ntechnical changes and convey them in understandable and commonly  under-\nstood  language. This legislation will make it easier for small business\nowners to have one resource to consult regarding  changes  the  law  and\nregulations.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect one year after it shall become a law.\n",
